in this buffer.

431
00:18:12,460 --> 00:18:15,505
But if you return something
between zero and count,

432
00:18:15,505 --> 00:18:18,475
that means that only a portion

433
00:18:18,475 --> 00:18:21,685
of the total amount
requested has been returned.

434
00:18:21,685 --> 00:18:23,920
There's a partial read rule here

435
00:18:23,920 --> 00:18:25,930
that determines how you can use

436
00:18:25,930 --> 00:18:27,550
this that's really helpful for

437
00:18:27,550 --> 00:18:28,600
our implementation and we'll

438
00:18:28,600 --> 00:18:30,490
talk about it in the next slide.

439
00:18:30,490 --> 00:18:32,545
If you return zero here,

440
00:18:32,545 --> 00:18:34,150
that means that
there's end of file

441
00:18:34,150 --> 00:18:36,925
and no data read
into the buffer.

442
00:18:36,925 --> 00:18:38,935
That's appropriate when you've

443
00:18:38,935 --> 00:18:40,840
completely exhausted
the content in

444
00:18:40,840 --> 00:18:43,270
your circular buffer and

445
00:18:43,270 --> 00:18:44,950
there's nothing left
to send to the user,

446
00:18:44,950 --> 00:18:46,690
which could be that
you've already read

447
00:18:46,690 --> 00:18:49,700
everything out or there was
nothing there to begin with.

448
00:18:49,770 --> 00:18:53,620
You'd want to return
