amount of data available.

464
00:19:41,710 --> 00:19:44,200
A way that you can
simplify the logic for

465
00:19:44,200 --> 00:19:46,900
Assignment 8 is you can
define the amount that

466
00:19:46,900 --> 00:19:48,865
you're going to return
from your read function

467
00:19:48,865 --> 00:19:52,315
as one single circular
buffer entry.

468
00:19:52,315 --> 00:19:55,120
This means that the offset

469
00:19:55,120 --> 00:19:57,100
is going to tell us
which entry we're in,

470
00:19:57,100 --> 00:19:58,990
and we're just going
to read the content of

471
00:19:58,990 --> 00:20:01,510
that entry into the buffer

472
00:20:01,510 --> 00:20:03,700
and return the total number of

473
00:20:03,700 --> 00:20:07,010
size of bytes that we're
reading based on that offset.

474
00:20:07,160 --> 00:20:09,610
We'll make user space calls

475
00:20:09,610 --> 00:20:12,110
again to read from
the next offset.

476
00:20:12,110 --> 00:20:14,590
As long as you
update this pointer

477
00:20:14,590 --> 00:20:17,480
correctly to point
to the next offset,

478
00:20:17,480 --> 00:20:20,455
in this case the next command
in our circular buffer,

479
00:20:20,455 --> 00:20:21,910
then the user space will

480
00:20:21,910 --> 00:20:23,625
just keep calling
your read function,

481
00:20:23,625 --> 00:20:26,045
keep reading one command
after another until it

482
00:20:26,045 --> 00:20:28,915
reads your entire circular
buffer content out.

483
00:20:28,915 --> 00:20:30,700
It turns out this can
make our logic and

484
00:20:30,700 --> 00:20:32,945
our driver quite a bit
easier to implement.

485
00:20:32,945 --> 00:20:36,010
I encourage you to look
at how to implement

486
00:20:36,010 --> 00:20:37,960
the partial read
rule and just read

487
00:20:37,960 --> 00:20:41,840
one command at a time
from your driver.

488
00:20:41,840 --> 00:20:44,130
This will be transparent from

489
00:20:44,130 --> 00:20:47,185
the perspective of using
a command like cat

490
00:20:47,185 --> 00:20:49,295
or fread from user space

491
00:20:49,295 --> 00:20:51,640
because all this
will be handled in

492
00:20:51,640 --> 00:20:53,020
the background for you by

493
00:20:53,020 --> 00:20:57,260
the multiple calls
to the read command.

494
00:20:58,230 --> 00:21:00,400
For a right implementation,

495
00:21:00,400 --> 00:21:01,265
we're going to do this slightly

496
00:21:01,265 --> 00:21:02,170
different than you normally

497
00:21:02,170 --> 00:21:04,280
would if you were implementing
write and driver.

498
00:21:04,280 --> 00:21:06,700
Normally, this file
position would point to

499
00:21:06,700 --> 00:21:09,365
the location where the write
should start in the buffer.

500
00:21:09,365 --> 00:21:11,650
But to make things
simple in Assignment 8,

501
00:21:11,650 --> 00:21:12,875
we're just going to ignore that.

502
00:21:12,875 --> 00:21:16,000
Always, every write is
either going to append to

503
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:17,455
the command that's being written

504
00:21:17,455 --> 00:21:19,610
when there's no
new line received,

505
00:21:19,610 --> 00:21:23,860
or it's going to write
the entire string

506
00:21:23,860 --> 00:21:25,325
into the command buffer whenever

507
00:21:25,325 --> 00:21:28,180
a new line is received as
a part of the command.

508
00:21:29,150 --> 00:21:31,480
Similar with write,
we need to think

509
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:33,545
about what our return
values are going to be.

510
00:21:33,545 --> 00:21:36,295
If the return value equals
the count that you specified,

511
00:21:36,295 --> 00:21:38,150
you're telling the
user space that

512
00:21:38,150 --> 00:21:39,875
you've written the
requested number

513
00:21:39,875 --> 00:21:41,175
of bytes successfully.

514
00:21:41,175 --> 00:21:43,685
If it's positive, but
smaller than count,

515
00:21:43,685 --> 00:21:45,605
then that means that
only part of it's been

516
00:21:45,605 --> 00:21:47,110
written and you're telling

517
00:21:47,110 --> 00:21:49,225
user space to retry the command.

518
00:21:49,225 --> 00:21:51,165
If you return zero,

519
00:21:51,165 --> 00:21:53,260
you're telling user space
that nothing's been written

520
00:21:53,260 --> 00:21:54,970
and user space is

521
00:21:54,970 --> 00:21:57,335
probably going to retry
the command in that case.

522
00:21:57,335 --> 00:21:59,350
This is most often

523
00:21:59,350 --> 00:22:01,745
seen when you're first
bringing up the application.

524
00:22:01,745 --> 00:22:03,485
You're saying, why
is my application

525
00:22:03,485 --> 00:22:05,780
hanging when I attempt
to do the first write?

526
00:22:05,780 --> 00:22:08,130
It's probably that you're
not returning the right size

527
00:22:08,130 --> 00:22:11,700
here in your
aesd_write function.

528
00:22:11,700 --> 00:22:14,160
You can use a
negative return here

529
00:22:14,160 --> 00:22:16,570
to indicate that an
error occurred, ENOMEM,

530
00:22:16,570 --> 00:22:18,095
if you weren't able to allocate

531
00:22:18,095 --> 00:22:19,565
memory or EFAULT if there was

532
00:22:19,565 --> 00:22:23,920
some other thing that occurred
that you can't handle.

533
00:22:24,050 --> 00:22:26,420
Talking a little bit
more specifically

534
00:22:26,420 --> 00:22:27,690
about the drivertest script

535
00:22:27,690 --> 00:22:30,640
that we'll use to verify
